hexagon: htp: the 32x2 dot adds the k-tiles in the order of the 32x1 dot
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-htp

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

The 32x2 dots of Q4_0, Q4_1, Q8_0, IQ4_NL and MXFP4 (hvx-mm-kernels-tiled.h)
do two k-tiles in each step, and they add the two scaled tile sums first:
acc + (s_a + s_b). The 32x1 dot, the multirow dots and the CPU reference add
them one after the other: (acc + s_a) + s_b. The two orders round
differently, thus each column of the 32x2 dot differs in the last bits from
the 32x1 dot of the same activation row. In the v79 simulator (k = 2048, 128
weight rows) 210 of 256 Q8_0 outputs and 190 of 256 Q4_0 outputs differ in
bits, and the 32x1 dot gives the bits of the sequential reference for each
output. The app graphs do not reach this condition with the default settings:
for Q8_0 and Q4_0 at 2 to 4 activation rows the matmul op takes the multirow
dots, and above 4 rows the HMX. The 32x2 dot runs with GGML_HEXAGON_MULTIROW=0
and for the other weight types.

This patch adds the two tile sums to the accumulator one after the other.
Then each column of the 32x2 dot gives the bits of the 32x1 dot (0 of 256
outputs differ for Q8_0 and for Q4_0). The simulator gives fewer cycles for
each 32x32 weight tile: 92.6 in place of 106.8 for Q8_0 at 2 rows, 185.1 in
place of 213.6 at 4 rows, and 98.1 in place of 108.9 for Q4_0 at 2 rows.

